WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.163
The second reason to act is that the
external clocks are already running,

2
00:00:04.163 --> 00:00:06.563
whether or not a network
program is funded.

3
00:00:06.563 --> 00:00:12.715
FIPS 140-2 certificates moved to NIST's
historical list on September 22,

4
00:00:12.715 --> 00:00:17.199
so new purchases should
state the 140-3 expectation.

5
00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:25.837
The post-quantum memo, M-26-15, wanted
agency migration plans by October 22, 2026.

6
00:00:25.837 --> 00:00:31.999
The first agency logging plan
under M-26-14 is due November 18.

7
00:00:31.999 --> 00:00:38.827
BOD 26-04's third phase lands December
7, FedRAMP's 2026 rules become

8
00:00:38.827 --> 00:00:46.150
mandatory on January 1, and the EIS
first option period ends July 31, 2027.

9
00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:50.413
The one that spends money is BOD 26-02.

10
00:00:50.413 --> 00:00:54.044
It requires identified end-of-support edge

11
00:00:54.044 --> 00:00:57.775
devices off agency networks
by August 5, 2027.

12
00:00:57.775 --> 00:00:59.918
I'll come back to it,
because it's the fork

13
00:00:59.918 --> 00:01:02.463
in the road for the whole
branch architecture.

14
00:01:02.463 --> 00:01:03.799
Here's the honest part.

15
00:01:03.799 --> 00:01:08.102
Three of these requirements fall inside
roughly fifteen months, and they all

16
00:01:08.102 --> 00:01:12.337
draw on the same network and security
staff a branch-edge program would need.

17
00:01:12.337 --> 00:01:15.401
An agency promising an
SD-WAN program next year

18
00:01:15.401 --> 00:01:18.700
alongside them is promising
the same people twice.

19
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.462
Say that plainly in the plan.

20
00:01:20.462 --> 00:01:23.411
And one clock has already run out:

21
00:01:23.411 --> 00:01:27.888
the legacy telecom contracts
ended by May 31, 2026.

22
00:01:27.888 --> 00:01:30.290
Next, what happens if you don't act.
